<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The US is in the throes of a major politic=
al backlash against technology. We=E2=80=99ve talked in this newsletter bef=
ore about the hatred of the hyperscale datacenters, which power artificial =
intelligence and have blanketed the US. And now there=E2=80=99s a secondary=
 wave of contempt sweeping the nation.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Cameras =E2=80=93 automated license plate =
readers, to be specific =E2=80=93 made by Flock Safety are inspiring ire an=
d violence across the country. These cameras snap pictures of car=E2=80=99s=
 license plates as they drive by. According to Flock, about 120,000 cameras=
 erected across the US conduct 2bn scans of license plates per month.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Like datacenters, the cameras offer a phys=
ical object to resist, unlike a social network or a piece of software. It=
=E2=80=99s quite a lot easier to protest a gigantic ugly building or an omi=
nous black camera on the side of the road than it is to give up Instagram.<=
/p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Widespread video surveillance in the US is=
n=E2=80=99t entirely new. In the aftermath of the terror attacks of 11 Sept=
ember 2001, the US government vastly increased the scope of its surveillanc=
e of telephonic and digital communications. Flock now offers local law enfo=
rcement a nationwide searchable database of license plate scans, and police=
 officers have been <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/=
c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR7l0i3omFT-LEnbcI1ckSgVec6y0BNm-LOESkhfw0KB5rmwV=
s1V1TOd_mvbNka4nzLrO2WuzJK-PBT7Hb0a_VfzvaF83Rv4B6UDmxKaLkTLmRpQ1qqRNK90tg2C=
8aV4MOYKbJSfadbQDCLdlnvcAw82u86SxG1_cJ_Uw6j-UdfSy-tlonWSwbZwE_g61tvpPfe_TkY=
NGJN7PG5pjoQy3L2M8C9XtjuuQ8OGhpRRNzixwPtoFzOF2rXlisY97oMe3Hx0--HMBVK74YsWk3=
0uaPKf6c4IdgWIE7aGI72V-BwqB/4tv/EU0_3BvHTH6Cu8s803_lfg/h38/h001.0W2bWfO2XrZ=
nB3Q8xetF07NmpWdClQukJ559cmnENy0" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">documented abus=
ing it egregiously</a> to stalk their romantic partners.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In the UK an estimated <a href=3D"https://=
ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRzcQ904TH2TS=
VFO56yXuASR6eV-wzpyiPyZC4LDhK0M9NqcQLUu_p3i3oVMYfQGMgsoIUhl8_9fytNXZG7hb8Tn=
sVwJ5r8dpgE1UCycJD1MhKFfYNxfJUSeGXLqpN-ec_RQ4HQ6G0aWRPIVCO-lhG2CcKA4gp2k5jQ=
iFig56H8N5b-mXvXaZpKIzII3GBALMXnM9eq7hC0Ck47V6wRIUA7q5-fU3WCikuocSTgpo56_Wr=
Uund_25tSwImUvGSEKZCg/4tv/EU0_3BvHTH6Cu8s803_lfg/h39/h001.4ICi5F7BYvZLyNcbA=
8Qf_mCUDq1kMQa6VdxYfV54IB0" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">1m surveillance camer=
as are operational</a>, and they have not drawn a similar level of politica=
l heat.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">London erected the =E2=80=9Cring of steel=
=E2=80=9D surveillance cordon, which included the wide deployment of CCTV c=
ameras, in the early 1990s, in response to bombings by the Provisional Iris=
h Republican Army.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Both countries went through their surveill=
ance evolutions roughly a decade apart. Those 10 years saw rapid advances i=
n technology that defined the public spaces that governments wanted watched=
 =E2=80=93 physical town squares or the digital one =E2=80=93 and growing a=
wareness of the profound impact of tech surveillance on the general public.=
 Simply put, the UK went through its transformation at a time when surveill=
ance by tech companies was not top of mind. And Americans aren=E2=80=99t ac=
customed to cameras on every corner the way Londoners are. We=E2=80=99re mo=
re likely to joke that each of us has a <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial=
.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.TXQfNyEMZ_311gAJD03AegPE6jv_7duKUxKPUuZ80JsSRcJD=
f-tSeDWaosnkNPGGCCc452JW8j_48OupcElm9L0skeTaqVkoQpxLvHfm_267JAvG1iKZi1RRCMU=
63VRiFO2aF-PdkRm9btuL7NTy00qwfBMEsK5opWxvx2D4JFqNMN4P1h51zp5XW0ZGWmLPI7t6a8=
tSGfFK7D7quuz97ulbAWRf5zCbUQ5eHch7aIIGQYEuenuVTocWA4mQic2d8mARhBjye7ljaOxil=
uhPkQ/4tv/EU0_3BvHTH6Cu8s803_lfg/h40/h001.A1tlRJqqVQ6HqX62d8k0nU-bkAD5Vq8x7=
v37zfUk1Nk" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">personal FBI agent</a> monitoring our=
 search history.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In addition, there hasn=E2=80=99t been an =
era-defining violent incident on the scale of 9/11 or the IRA bombings that=
 would have softened the American public to cameras watching their cars. So=
 instead, <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_=
JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEDUZSC8KPzZutimV4n8d8SzFPAnVIgS67gxSd=
6KEvhW-WyNPJjrjjG7Mn7fzDST-vWC7wVAA4tl4AZHG30W67zRkSmvsZ_O2nd4doZ3k8jnke9qZ=
FrEM90YZn4qTuOaTqloWHuObFz9bHAj40tXhgD-0YsCm8aehv6-HjjrNZ5dkwToIdcZioag9AdE=
yFMw2oIUCM61Hop0Jv2Y4z0YtwEyAZcc-JyB1nCxGNObRdjdTfs0zpL9lHOx0Vh7utzfnRs/4tv=
/EU0_3BvHTH6Cu8s803_lfg/h41/h001.Cb1hGmPVllzYg1rZiIJSZIMTfwiRL5bCoc6xG_jCH6=
U" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">Americans are destroying them for fun</a>.</p>
